From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Thu, 17 Nov 94 19:06:03 EST
Subject: [#94-11-261] ENVIRONMENT: Nature's real plan 

Nancy Lebovitz says:
>Nitpick alert: The most recent theory I've heard about the moderately
>far future of earth is that it won't be engulfed by the sun--the sun
>will have blown off enough mass by becoming a red giant that the
>earth's orbit will expand too far for the surface of the sun to 
>reach it.

Two months ago there was a discussion of this topic on this list.  There was
discussion of the solar wind, the possible change in solar mass, the
possible change in Earth orbit, etc. However, at the time the discussion did
not seem definitive to me.  Therefore, I used Tipler's analysis from his
book which came out this year.  I would be interested in references to
alternative theories. This does highlight the fact that these theories 
do have a speculative component. 

In examining two month old messages I now note that Peter McCluskey also used
the same Tipler quote.  I also note that Minsky offered the danger of a Solar
envelopment of Earth and of an asteroid strike as reasons to explore space.
Let me acknowledge these messages now.  Minsky also mentioned the dangers of
nanotech plagues or unstoppable epidemics as other motivations to establish
outposts off Earth.  Alexander Chislenko mentioned that solar processes
could be interfered with to alter stellar evolution. Ed Regis discusses
playing with the Sun in ``Great Mambo Chicken and the Transhuman Condition''.

The main orientation of my message was intended to be different from this
earlier valuable thread.  I was arguing that it does not make sense to
sentimentalize ``nature'' or valorize a ``natural order'' without humanity
(transhumanity or posthumanity) when it seems likely that the ``natural
order'' will obliterate the biosphere unless humans intervene.
        
        Gregory Sullivan


