From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Thu, 17 Nov 94 23:13:24 EST
Subject: [#94-11-264] POLITICS: Pragmatic transition

robj@netcom.com (Rob Jellinghaus) says:
>... to the extent that Extropians (and more
>generally, libertarians) want to tear down the government NOW without
>simultaneously constructing voluntary institutions which could perform
>the functions (education, etc) that most people associate positively
>with government, they alienate people who think that continuing those
>functions is more important than a fundamentalist moral outrage at
>coercive taxation.  

I personally think your political point should be taken seriously.  It
suggests a challenge for libertarians to come up with plausible mechanisms
which allow a transition from our current society towards a society that
more closely embodies libertarian ideals. I think this challenge is worth
thinking about.

Some libertarians apparently think organizing a militia in preparation for
civil war is the best strategy. Others envision an electronic Blacknet which
will somehow force society to become anarchocapitalist or perhaps just an
anarchy.  Still others try to ignore governments and create their own
libertarian microcosm. I think these strategies are flawed but I will not
argue that here.  The Libertarian Party has not been effective so far in
electing significant numbers of candidates.  That may change.  Meanwhile why
not try to popularize the following mechanism for transition.

The idea is to allow taxpayers much more complete control over the
distribution of the taxes that they pay.  There would be multiple private or
non-profit programs each of which performs some service which is considered
socially desirable:

1) Multiple welfare or workfare programs
2) A panoply of schools
3) Multiple space exploration programs
4) Multiple police/military forces
etc.

These organizations will compete for the pool of donations which are mandated
by the government. A taxpayer must pay some minimum lump sum into the
programs overall but he can reallocate his donations according to some
periodic schedule.  The organizations regularly report back to their
sponsors and give details of their myriad accomplishments. They try to
convince givers to give again and they can also advertise to find new
sponsors.

This system is still coercive but it is a movement towards a non-coercive
system and it begins to build the foundation for anarchocapitalism.
Networks of computers would allow the collection, processing and
dissemination of the information required for this system.  (I have been
told there is a science fiction story which outlines a similar idea.  If you
know of such a story please let me know.)

There are many potential problems with this scheme and many details which
must be worked out:

Which programs are socially desirable? Taxpayer Fred may wish to give to the
``Free beers for Fred foundation''. There will probably be a method to
choose candidate programs which excludes this this kind of foundation.

How will programs be coordinated? If there are multiple programs won't there
be duplication? Initially, the number of programs in an area can be
restricted. For example, there may be one space program. But the program may
still allow the giver to target his funds to specific projects.  Over the
longer term multiple programs can be allowed and they can report to donors
on their attempts at cooperation. Donors can decide on how much cooperation
they value.

An un-unified police or military is seemingly problematic but
anarchocapitalists think it will work.  Initially, in this scheme each force
would have to agree to enforce the taxation system or to allow its
enforcement by others.

Will the computer network entail to much centralization of power?  What
about the privacy of choices? The tools of modern cryptography can help to
reduce these problems.

What if a fickle public causes wide variations in funding from year to year
on programs? Initially, percentage bounds on changes in allocations could be
enforced. In the long run organizations could simply accept fluctuations or
attempt to sign multiyear contracts with taxpayers.

What about coercive laws against drugs and so called victimless crimes?
Perhaps donors will refuse to give money to enforce these laws.

There are many other concerns which I will skip now to consider a key
question.  Is this really a transition mechanism to a more libertarian
society? Well the theory is that the scheme will work so well that the
mandatory contributions will be reduced over time until all contributions
are voluntary.

The scheme does in my opinion give more liberty to taxpayers than the current
system and if it is well implemented I think it is preferable.

robj@netcom.com (Rob Jellinghaus) says:
>Yes, I know that Extropians support private
>schooling, charitable donations, etc.  My point is that that support
>will need to be channeled into a cohesive framework of private
>institutions ready to come online simultaneously with the proposed
>eradication of government, before people will realistically perceive
>that things won't fall apart with no public sector.  I think a good
>many libertarians would rather get the tax man out of their lives NOW,
>and let the rest sort itself out later.  And that's why they are, and
>will remain, a fringe party.

The above scheme tries to build the replacement framework.  Gregory Sullivan

